course: dictionary
lesson: 7738
title: Pulsars: cosmic lighthouses
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: A pulsar is a dead star that spins very fast and sends out beams of radio waves or light. «Spinning ones sweep radio beams past the Earth like a lighthouse.» A lighthouse turns its light round and round, so it seems to flash when the beam passes you. A pulsar does the same with its beams.
explain: Pulsar is a noun, so it names a thing: a pulsar, pulsars. Its beams reach Earth as very regular pulses. A pulse is a steady beat, like your heartbeat, so a pulsar sends out regular beats of radio waves or light.
explain: Use the context to work out the hard words. The sentence says the collapsed core is «only about 20 km across, as wide as a city, yet heavier than the Sun». So tiny means very small, and heavy here means it has a huge amount of matter packed into a small space.
fact: A pulsar is a neutron star, the tiny, very heavy core left after a giant star explodes in a supernova.
fact: «Pulsar» means a tiny, very heavy, spinning dead star that sends out beams of radio waves or light.
fact: The beams reach Earth as very regular pulses, like a lighthouse flashing.
fact: Scientists use pulsars like cosmic clocks, because their pulses are so regular.
fact: In 1967 Jocelyn Bell Burnell, a student from Northern Ireland, discovered pulsars.
exercise: «A pulsar is a tiny, very heavy, spinning dead star.» What kind of thing is a pulsar? | A planet with rings | *A spinning dead star | A giant red sun | A cloud of gas
exercise: «Spinning ones sweep radio beams past the Earth like a lighthouse.» What do the beams do to Earth? | They stay still and never move | They block the light from the Sun | They melt the ice on Earth | *They sweep past Earth, so we see regular pulses
exercise: Why do scientists use pulsars like cosmic clocks? | *Because their pulses are very regular | Because they are close to the Sun | Because they glow pink | Because they are slow and random
test: Who discovered pulsars in 1967? | Isaac Newton | Marie Curie | Galileo Galilei | *Jocelyn Bell Burnell
test: «Its pulses are so regular that scientists use them like cosmic clocks.» What does regular mean here? | Loud and angry | Random and messy | *Happening at steady, even times | Hidden from view
exam: A pulsar sends out beams that sweep past Earth. Why does it seem to blink? | It is far too small to see | *Its beams pass Earth in regular pulses, like a lighthouse | It is blinking on and off on purpose | It is covered by clouds
